DB structure(this is already exist in the db)-

CREATE TABLE public.rooms (
  room_id integer NOT NULL DEFAULT nextval('rooms_room_id_seq'::regclass),
  room_name text NOT NULL,
  room_type text,
  room_status text,
  CONSTRAINT rooms_pkey PRIMARY KEY (room_id)
);

CREATE TABLE public.subjects (
  subject_id integer NOT NULL DEFAULT nextval('subjects_subject_id_seq'::regclass),
  subject_code text,
  subject_course_no text,
  department_id integer,
  subject_section text,
  subject_descriptive_title text,
  subject_units real,
  subject_lec_hrs real,
  subject_lab_hrs real,
  subject_status text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'::text,
  mth_schedule text,
  tfs_schedule text,
  mth_room text,
  tfs_room text,
  curr_id integer,
  is_general boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false,
  CONSTRAINT subjects_pkey PRIMARY KEY (subject_id),
  CONSTRAINT subjects_department_id_fkey FOREIGN KEY (department_id) REFERENCES public.departments(department_id)
);

CREATE TABLE public.automatic_scheduler (
  id integer NOT NULL DEFAULT nextval('course_offerings_id_seq'::regclass),
  CONSTRAINT automatic_scheduler_pkey PRIMARY KEY (id),
  CONSTRAINT automatic_scheduler_department_id_fkey FOREIGN KEY (department_id) REFERENCES public.departments(department_id),
  curr_id integer,
  code text,
  course_no text,
  department_id integer,
  section text,
  descriptive_title text,
  units real,
  lec_hrs real,
  lab_hrs real,
  mth_schedule text,
  mth_room_id text CHECK (mth_room_id IS NULL OR mth_room_id ~ '^[0-9]+(\/[0-9]+)*$'::text),
  tfs_schedule text,
  tfs_room_id text CHECK (tfs_room_id IS NULL OR tfs_room_id ~ '^[0-9]+(\/[0-9]+)*$'::text),
  merged text DEFAULT false
);


================================================================================
GENETIC ALGORITHM - AUTOMATIC SCHEDULING
================================================================================

Genetic Algorithm — Automatic Schedule (Handles room and time slot assignment only)

AVAILABLE DAYS
  Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday
  (Wednesday excluded — Saturday is user-defined only, not automated)

DAY PATTERNS
  MTH — Monday, Thursday (automated)
  TF — Tuesday, Friday (automated)
  SAT — Saturday (user-defined only, rarely assigned)

TIME BOUNDARY
  Earliest Start: 7:30 AM
  Latest End: 8:00 PM (to be confirmed)

================================================================================
PRE-SCHEDULING FILTERS (Applied once before GA runs)
================================================================================
PRE-1 — All subjects are included in the automated scheduling regardless of subject_status. The GA targets and fixes subjects with missing fields or conflicted schedules. Only merged subjects are disregarded.
PRE-2 — Remove all rooms where room_status = inactive.
PRE-3 — General subjects (is_general = true) are excluded from automated scheduling. Left blank for user to fill manually.
PRE-4 — Merged subjects are detected before GA runs. Schedule and room assignment are left as-is for merged subjects. Only flagged for review if conflict is detected. 
MERGED SUBJECT DETECTION LOGIC 
  if (Course Number AND Descriptive Title == Course Number AND Descriptive Title)
    if (Schedule AND Room Assignment == Schedule AND Room Assignment)
       Row = Merged →  disregard in schedule automation, leave as-is
ELSE
    Row = Conflict → flag for review

================================================================================
ROOM CONSTRAINTS (R)
================================================================================

HARD CONSTRAINTS
R-H1 — No Overlapping Times: Two classes or events cannot be assigned to the exact same room at the same time.
R-H2 — Laboratory type subjects have priority over laboratory type rooms. Lecture subjects are assigned to lecture rooms first. If lecture rooms are exhausted, lecture subjects may overflow into laboratory rooms.
R-H3 — Laboratory rooms can also serve as lecture rooms but are always taken first by laboratory type subjects.
R-H4 — A subject can have both laboratory and lecture hours assigned to the same room within the same MTH or TF schedule if needed.
R-H5 — A room with room_status = inactive must not be assigned to any class.

SOFT CONSTRAINTS

R-S1 — Prefer distributing room usage evenly across all available active rooms to avoid overloading a single room.

================================================================================
SUBJECT CONSTRAINTS (S)
================================================================================

HARD CONSTRAINTS
S-H1 — Classes can only be scheduled on Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, and Friday. Saturday is excluded from automation and is user-defined only.
S-H2 — A subject assigned to MTH pattern can only be scheduled on Monday and Thursday.
S-H3 — A subject assigned to TF pattern can only be scheduled on Tuesday and Friday. Saturday is excluded from automation.
S-H4 — Classes must start no earlier than 7:30 AM.
S-H5 — Classes must end no later than 8:00 PM (to be confirmed).
S-H6 — A subject with subject_lec_hrs greater than 0 must be assigned a lecture room first. If lecture rooms are exhausted it may overflow to a laboratory room.
S-H7 — A subject with subject_lab_hrs greater than 0 must be assigned a laboratory room with priority. Laboratory rooms are taken first by lab type subjects.
S-H8 — Lecture hours and laboratory hours are always split equally across the two days of the pattern based on max hours (subject_lec_hrs + subject_lab_hrs). Each day gets half of the total hours. Example: 3 hrs on MTH = 1.5 hrs Monday + 1.5 hrs Thursday.
S-H9 — Subject hours must never split across MTH and TF patterns. All hours of a subject stay within its assigned pattern. Exception: user may manually override. While inputting, the system must detect and flag conflicts automatically in real time.
S-H10 — The GA targets all subjects including those with missing fields or conflicted schedules for fixing. Refer to PRE-1.
S-H11 — The same section must not have two subjects scheduled at the same time on the same day (section conflict).
S-H12 — Schedule splitting is always applied. Hours are divided equally across the two days of the pattern. Example: Max hours = 3 on MTH → Monday gets 1.5 hrs, Thursday gets 1.5 hrs. By end of week the total quota of 3 hrs is met.

SOFT CONSTRAINTS
S-S1 — [HIGH] Prioritize scheduling time slots as close to 7:30 AM as possible. Earlier time slots should be filled first before pushing classes later in the day to avoid unnecessary late scheduling.

S-S3 — [HIGH] Hours are always divided equally across the two days of the assigned pattern only (MTH = Mon + Thu, TF = Tue + Fri). Never split across MTH and TF.
S-S4 — [LOW] There should be at least a 30-minute break between consecutive classes for the same section.
S-S5 — [MEDIUM] A section should not exceed 5 consecutive hours of class in a single day without a break.
S-S6 — [MEDIUM] A section should not have more than 10 hours of total class time in a single day.
S-S7 — [MEDIUM] Subjects with both lecture and laboratory components should prefer lecture on the first day and lab on the second day of the pattern. This can be swapped vice versa to give room opening to lab or lecture rooms. Example: Subject A → Monday Lecture Room 1 / Thursday Lab Room 2 Subject B → Monday Lab Room 2 / Thursday Lecture Room 1
S-S8 — [LOW] Prefer scheduling major or difficult subjects earlier in the day.

S-S10 — [LOW] Subjects under the same curriculum (curr_id) should be spread across the week rather than packed into a single day.
S-S11 — [LOW] Prefer distributing a section's subjects evenly between MTH and TF patterns to balance the student's weekly load.
S-S12 — [LOW] Avoid assigning all heavy or major subjects to the same pattern for a given section.




